tion/validate]} {:db/id 17592186104482 :answer-field/correct "answer-92f1534b-0b67-5f94-9e15-ecb679bfea21" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8232f384-73db-579c-a4ce-03209669d5f2" "answer-ecfdc436-1de4-515e-9fe8-d1325e32d006" "answer-f6645f46-3bda-5f69-a00b-a79771a6c61c" "answer-c5d6824c-bcc8-510e-9ae1-ae28f2c136c6" "answer-92f1534b-0b67-5f94-9e15-ecb679bfea21"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8232f384-73db-579c-a4ce-03209669d5f2" :answer/id #uuid "8232f384-73db-579c-a4ce-03209669d5f2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\overset{―}{AB}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ecfdc436-1de4-515e-9fe8-d1325e32d006" :answer/id #uuid "ecfdc436-1de4-515e-9fe8-d1325e32d006" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\overset{―}{BD}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f6645f46-3bda-5f69-a00b-a79771a6c61c" :answer/id #uuid "f6645f46-3bda-5f69-a00b-a79771a6c61c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\overset{―}{BE}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c5d6824c-bcc8-510e-9ae1-ae28f2c136c6" :answer/id #uuid "c5d6824c-bcc8-510e-9ae1-ae28f2c136c6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\overset{―}{CD}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-92f1534b-0b67-5f94-9e15-ecb679bfea21" :answer/id #uuid "92f1534b-0b67-5f94-9e15-ecb679bfea21" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\overset{―}{CE}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-688c82f3-42f6-5fe7-994b-b325cfd9eb38" :question/id #uuid "688c82f3-42f6-5fe7-994b-b325cfd9eb38" :question/math-academy-id "q-52823" :question/problem "How many diagonals does a quadrilateral $ABCD$ have?" :question/worked-solution "Diagonals join non-adjacent vertices.\n\nAs we can see from the diagram below, the diagonals of the quadrilateral are $\\overset{―}{AC}$ and $\\overset{―}{BD}.$\n\n![](images/90/9010725d2b0e260cf7ef71eee5adb07b12346727eeffbf8c1fab3efc07310e22.png)\n\nTherefore, the quadrilateral $ABCD$ has $2$ diagonals." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-795e7210-013a-555f-b122-6a0c7be21919"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-795e7210-013a-555f-b122-6a0c7be21919" :answer-field/id #uuid "795e7210-013a-555f-b122-6a0c7be21919" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-686b4cae-4ac1-590b-9831-48d4bb7d3761" :answer-field/choices ["answer-63b93212-7687-5a37-8927-6748f684fd36" "answer-686b4cae-4ac1-590b-9831-48d4bb7d3761" "answer-4f4904c5-3ef6-5aa3-93de-ebb311d1db42" "answer-e725f9d1-e205-585e-a9b1-9211c3693eed" "answer-0101c798-7940-555f-9b8a-758b31da095c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-63b93212-7687-5a37-8927-6748f684fd36" :answer/id #uuid "63b93212-7687-5a37-8927-6748f684fd36" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-686b4cae-4ac1-590b-9831-48d4bb7d3761" :answer/id #uuid "686b4cae-4ac1-590b-9831-48d4bb7d3761" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4f4904c5-3ef6-5aa3-93de-ebb311d1db42" :answer/id #uuid "4f4904c5-3ef6-5aa3-93de-ebb311d1db42" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e725f9d1-e205-585e-a9b1-9211c3693eed" :answer/id #uuid "e725f9d1-e205-585e-a9b1-9211c3693eed" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0101c798-7940-555f-9b8a-758b31da095c" :answer/id #uuid "0101c798-7940-555f-9b8a-758b31da095c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-b3f2064e-89fd-57bf-9cbf-30cae7770c78" :question/id #uuid "b3f2064e-89fd-57bf-9cbf-30cae7770c78" :question/math-academy-id "q-117973" :question/problem "![](images/c2/c268f171428feb3373b8c36f21fa7d9dfc25d2624bb313ffab272fca5459a727.png)\n\nWhat are the elements $\\overset{―}{BC}$ and $\\overset{―}{DF}$ for the polygon above?" :question/worked-solution "The segment $\\overset{―}{BC}$ joins two adjacent vertices. Thus, $\\overset{―}{BC}$ is a side of the polygon.\n\nThe segment $\\overset{―}{DF}$ joins two non-adjacent vertices. Therefore, $\\overset{―}{DF}$ is a diagonal." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4ca7cd59-aef1-56ab-a2be-27bd5a477ef2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4ca7cd59-aef1-56ab-a2be-27bd5a477ef2" :answer-field/id #uuid "4ca7cd59-aef1-56ab-a2be-27bd5a477ef2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-32da3b54-b750-5887-b04f-86b7ef454ce8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-bb02e2b0-9f14-5741-8d89-64ad6c2f7574" "answer-2dfad3b8-82ab-5de4-8e98-222d1152b2c3" "answer-982fc555-abad-5f37-97a2-a48637da23fb" "answer-5f349821-bdfc-55ec-a987-32576027742f" "answer-32da3b54-b750-5887-b04f-86b7ef454ce8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bb02e2b0-9f14-5741-8d89-64ad6c2f7574" :answer/id #uuid "bb02e2b0-9f14-5741-8d89-64ad6c2f7574" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$\\overset{―}{BC}$ and $\\overset{―}{BF}$ are both sides" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2dfad3b8-82ab-5de4-8e98-222d1152b2c3" :answer/id #uuid "2dfad3b8-82ab-5de4-8e98-222d1152b2c3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$\\overset{―}{BC}$ and $\\overset{―}{DF}$ are both diagonals" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-982fc555-abad-5f37-97a2-a48637da23fb" :answer/id #uuid "982fc555-abad-5f37-97a2-a48637da23fb" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$\\overset{―}{BC}$ is a diagonal and $\\overset{―}{DF}$ is a side" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5f349821-bdfc-55ec-a987-32576027742f" :answer/id #uuid "5f349821-bdfc-55ec-a987-32576027742f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$\\overset{―}{BC}$ is a diagonal and $\\overset{―}{DF}$ is a vertex" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-32da3b54-b750-5887-b04f-86b7ef454ce8" :answer/id #uuid "32da3b54-b750-5887-b04f-86b7ef454ce8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$\\overset{―}{BC}$ is a side and $\\overset{―}{DF}$ is a diagonal" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-9808b081-0611-54e0-8956-702438c9b60b" :question/id #uuid "9808b081-0611-54e0-8956-702438c9b60b" :question/math-academy-id "q-164607" :question/problem "How many diagonals does a pentagon $ABCDE$ have?" :question/worked-solution "Diagonals join non-adjacent vertices.\n\nAs we can see from the diagram below, the diagonals of the pentagon are $\\overset{―}{AC},$ $\\overset{―}{AD},$ $\\overset{―}{BD},$ $\\overset{―}{BE}$ and $\\overset{―}{CE}.$\n\n![](images/b3/b3956aebca9c5cf83a42b72de049d3a2c2e91604e336ae3d1d62ba2d8e22d315.png)\n\nTherefore, the pentagon $ABCDE$ has $5$ diagonals." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-692fd744-03c7-5427-919b-245ddf334c3a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-692fd744-03c7-5427-919b-245ddf334c3a" :answer-field/id #uuid "692fd744-03c7-5427-919b-245ddf334c3a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-da974571-d34d-56c4-bd1e-d81c82ad54dc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-32035f88-4386-5a0e-9a90-12c39c00ba8d" "answer-c43ab6a2-7a17-5b0a-be28-eb7ab27a2b15" "answer-2f5d5aff-dad6-5ecb-be8f-94ad8daa507d" "answer-da974571-d34d-56c4-bd1e-d81c82ad54dc" "answer-3e85c536-ba38-5d8c-901f-4e5bc71b071f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-32035f88-4386-5a0e-9a90-12c39c00ba8d" :answer/id #uuid "32035f88-4386-5a0e-9a90-12c39c00ba8d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c43ab6a2-7a17-5b0a-be28-eb7ab27a2b15" :answer/id #uuid "c43ab6a2-7a17-5b0a-be28-eb7ab27a2b15" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2f5d5aff-dad6-5ecb-be8f-94ad8daa507d" :answer/id #uuid "2f5d5aff-dad6-5ecb-be8f-94ad8daa507d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-da974571-d34d-56c4-bd1e-d81c82ad54dc" :answer/id #uuid "da974571-d34d-56c4-bd1e-d81c82ad54dc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3e85c536-ba38-5d8c-901f-4e5bc71b071f" :answer/id #uuid "3e85c536-ba38-5d8c-901f-4e5bc71b071f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186107178 :tutorial/content "The shape below consists of $6$ line segments defined by the $6$ points $A$, $B$, $C$, $D$, $E$, and $F.$ These line segments form a loop. \n\n![](images/25/259bfd166a7870bd1a057b28d4b3324f3b98d05222596f1426afa01d6573e3d8.png)\n\nA chain of straight line segments that form a loop is called a **polygon**.\n\nThe line segments are the **sides** of the polygon, and the endpoints of the sides are the **vertices**.\n\nFor this polygon:\n\n- The $6$ vertices are $A$, $B$, $C$, $D$, $E$, and $F.$\n\n- The $6$ sides are $\\overline{AB}$, $\\overline{BC}$, $\\overline{CD}$, $\\overline{DE}$, $\\overline{EF}$, and $\\overline{AF}.$\n\nTo denote a particular polygon, we start at one vertex and loop around the polygon, reading off the vertices in order. So, the polygon above can be denoted $ABCDEF.$\n\nThe singular form of the word *vertices* is **vertex**. So, $A$ is a vertex, $B$ is a vertex, etc." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186107179 :tutorial/content "The name of a polygon is determined by the number of sides it has. \n\nThe polygon below has $6$ sides. A six-sided polygon is called a **hexagon**.\n\n![](images/25/259bfd166a7870bd1a057b28d4b3324f3b98d05222596f1426afa01d6573e3d8.png)\n\nThe names of the polygons with up to ten sides are given below:\n\n| Number of sides | Name |\n| --- | --- |\n| $3$ | Triangle |\n| $4$ | Quadrilateral |\n| $5$ | Pentagon |\n| $6$ | Hexagon |\n| $7$ | Heptagon |\n| $8$ | Octagon |\n| $9$ | Nonagon |\n| $10$ | Decagon |" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186107182 :tutorial/content "We can categorize polygons in different ways.\n\nA polygon is **simple** if its boundary does not intersect itself. Otherwise, the polygon is **complex**.\n\n![](images/71/71ef7dbf87cb3a097ec153eb4c4e18920c2567e6a239a788f4340a0a5f33ea6f.png)\n\nA polygon is **convex** if it has no angles pointing inwards. Otherwise, if the polygon has angles pointing inwards, we call it **concave**.\n\n![](images/ef/ef7357c0e21760c3e7f159c253c527e35aac869ce16381132a53356dc53455e2.png)\n\nIt might help to remember that a con**cave** polygon looks a bit like the entrance to a **cave** when viewed from the outside. \n\n![](images/11/11e9844e49a5c586ec573e08865eb7d02dd68459e90df90749116a9eb1575430.png)\n\n**Note**: A more technical way to define a convex polygon is to say that all of its *internal angles* are less than $180^\\circ.$ If you're not sure what an internal angle is yet, don't worry! We'll learn more about the internal angles of a polygon in future lessons." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186107180 :tutorial/content "Consider the pentagon $ABCDE,$ shown below.\n\n![](images/b1/b11b509e881cd813ff6879a0a6fa9559d51ffcbc5da4c150a0eaf0a9ee82a159.png)\n\nWe have the following definitions: \n\n- Two *sides* are **adjacent** (or **consecutive**) if they have a common vertex.\n\nFor example, $\\color[rgb]{0.753,0.494,0.643}\\overline{BC}$ is adjacent to $\\color[rgb]{0.902, 0.624, 0}\\overline{AB}$ *and* ${\\color[rgb]{0.902, 0.624, 0}\\overline{CD}}.$\n\n![](images/76/760a9cbccd117214d4e93392cdae17bf117497501b05f7b2e25c6c8cb5cd43e1.png)\n\n- Two *vertices* are **adjacent** if there is a side joining them. \n\nFor example, the vertex $E$ is adjacent to the vertices $D$ and $A$.\n\n![](images/6d/6d857920609da14fb0b31f762400c5f54e9e428717c2b55fcb17215280bbbfed.png)\n\n- A **diagonal** is a line segment that joins two non-adjacent vertices. \n\nFor example, ${\\color[rgb]{0.902, 0.624, 0}{\\overline{AC}}}, {\\color[rgb]{0.902, 0.624, 0}{\\overline{BD}}},$ and ${\\color[rgb]{0.902, 0.624, 0}{\\overline{BE}}}$ are diagonals of our pentagon.\n\n![](images/ad/adf7afaa66144a33dba7ff3732b01619bc13cac0fae59c466393d4084c748a59.png)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105971 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
